Done. Here's what I did:

**Fixed 4 byte-index safety violations in test code** across 3 files, replacing unsafe `&str[..n] patterns with safe_truncate()`:

1. **`src/commands_project.rs:1717`** — &content[..200.min(content.len())] → crate::format::safe_truncate(&content, 200)
2. **`src/help.rs:2202** — &text[..text.len().min(60)] → crate::format::safe_truncate(text, 60)`
3. `src/tool_wrappers.rs:1792` — &text[..text.len().min(200)] → crate::format::safe_truncate(text, 200)
4. `src/tool_wrappers.rs:2031` — &text[..text.len().min(100)] → crate::format::safe_truncate(&text, 100)

These patterns would panic if the string contained multi-byte UTF-8 characters and the byte offset fell mid-character. While the test strings are currently ASCII, this violates the project's own safety rule in CLAUDE.md. safe_truncate handles char boundaries correctly using `is_char_boundary()`.

Also included: a leftover fix from Task 1 in prompt_retry.rs that makes a recovery hint test check semantics rather than exact wording.

